4985,-0.07762809842824936,-0.11470801383256912,-1.4543427823809907e-05,-0.001618624315597117,0.0,-6.282132380874828e-05,-0.07194748520851135,0.0,0.0,0.0,-0.09277117997407913,0.0,-1.2113115787506104,0.0,0.0,-0.00017975145601667464,-0.010398022830486298,-1.550431251525879,-0.011771737597882748,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-4.768370445162873e-07,-1.1920928244535389e-07,0.0,0.0,0.0,-0.00030489088385365903,0.0,-1.3708974620385561e-05,-7.056941103655845e-05],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,1359,18169,24609,17228,315,264,5792,12371,6113,448,12822,400,19,3,14924,1293,11,1378,3232,42939,42446,525,14122,11,438,6839,13,3555,374,279,3082,11,304,9334,14924,11,315,279,5537,429,374,4185,311,279,1378,42446,30,17399,697,4226,304,44548,17855,1352,13,508,6405,921,7633,1188,15,11,18,29621,7,15,11,23,29621,7,19,11,16,16,29621,7,23,11,23,29621,7,23,11,18,29621,7,19,11,15,29621,21621,11,11453,10,57212,7,16,1106,7633,1188,19,11,15,29621,7,15,11,23,29621,7,23,11,23,29621,21621,11,3691,51412,13571,10,57212,7,16,1106,7633,1188,15,11,18,29621,7,19,11,16,16,29621,7,23,11,18,29621,21621,11,3691,51412,15521,10,57212,7,16,1106,1502,445,19,44970,23,11,20,13,20,701,36,317,24157,6405,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nBy joining alternate vertices of a regular hexagon with edges $4$ inches long, two equilateral triangles are formed, as shown. What is the area, in square inches, of the region that is common to the two triangles? Express your answer in simplest radical form. [asy]\ndraw((0,3)--(0,8)--(4,11)--(8,8)--(8,3)--(4,0)--cycle,black+linewidth(1));\ndraw((4,0)--(0,8)--(8,8)--cycle, black+dashed+linewidth(1));\ndraw((0,3)--(4,11)--(8,3)--cycle, black+dotted+linewidth(1));\nlabel(\"4\",(8,5.5),E);\n[/asy]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"5b6e4fc7504f83664d0c9030035887b3adcc7132fbd93a6780ac61da635a28c2"},"seconds":28.079707384109497,"seed":155836707867363638,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we need to find the area of the region that is common to the two equilateral triangles formed by joining alternate vertices of a regular hexagon with edges of $4$ inches long.\n\n### Step 1: Determine the side length of the equilateral triangles\nSince the hexagon is regular and each side is $4$ inches long, the equilateral triangles formed by connecting alternate vertices will have sides that are the same as the distance between alternate vertices of the hexagon. This distance is equal to the side length of the hexagon, which is $4$ inches.\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the area of one equilateral triangle\nThe formula for the area of an equilateral triangle with side length $a$ is:\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}a^2 \\]\n\nFor our equilateral triangle, $a = 4$ inches. Plugging this into the formula:\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 4^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 16 = 4\\sqrt{3} \\]\n\n### Step 3: Determine the region common to both triangles\nThe two equilateral triangles intersect in a region that is itself an equilateral triangle with side length half the side length of the original triangles. This is because the intersection forms a smaller equilateral triangle whose vertices are the midpoints of the sides of the larger triangles.\n\nThe side length of this smaller equilateral triangle is:\n\\[ \\frac{4}{2} = 2 \\text{ inches} \\]\n\n### Step 4: Calculate the area of the smaller equilateral triangle\nUsing the area formula for an equilateral triangle again, but with $a = 2$ inches:\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 2^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 4 = \\sqrt{3} \\]\n\n### Final Answer\nThe area of the region that is common to the two equilateral triangles is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\sqrt{3}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
